// Runoff: vertical streaking without a transport solve.
//
// Wet Stain answers "where does the liquid go" by moving it, one texel per iteration, twenty-odd
// ping-ponged passes deep. That is the right shape for liquid that pools, converges and flows
// around things. It is a great deal of machinery for a dirty streak under a ledge, which does not
// converge or pool -- it goes down, it gets thinner, it stops.
//
// So this does not simulate anything. It prepares one source field, then smears that field along
// a single axis with a directional Gaussian, several times at slightly different lengths. No
// state, no ping-pong. Everything that makes it read as runoff rather than as a blur comes from
// the shaping around the smear: where the source is allowed to exist, how the reach varies with
// source strength, and how the strata disagree with each other.
//
// Four passes, and the split is deliberate at every seam.
//
// Downsample, Prepare and Resolve run on a fixed analysis grid; only Apply runs at the
// composition's own resolution. That is the whole reason this is affordable at 4K. Every distance
// in the effect is a UV fraction -- the streak reach, the cavity radii, the Gaussian sigma, the
// tap spacing -- so the analysis answers the same question at every composition size, and
// answering it sixteen times over at 4K buys sampling density that the fields being sampled do
// not contain. It cost sixteen times as much to produce the same streak, which is the wrong way
// round, and at 4K it was enough GPU time in one submission to trip the driver's watchdog.
//
// Downsample is its own pass because the cavity rings read the height twenty-six times per
// analysis texel. Point-sampling a 4K height from a 1K grid would alias every one of those taps
// and make the surface response at 4K a noisier answer than the same response at 1K -- and it
// would scatter twenty-six reads across a 32MB texture instead of across a 2MB one, which was the
// actual cost rather than the arithmetic. Box-filtering once, into a texture the rings then hit
// entirely in cache, fixes the fidelity and the locality together.
//
// Prepare is separate from Resolve because it is where the fractal noise is evaluated, and
// putting it in its own pass makes it structurally impossible to evaluate inside the Gaussian tap
// loop -- which, at twenty-four taps times five strata, is the difference between one FBM per
// pixel and a hundred and twenty. The alternative, one pass with the noise hoisted above the loop
// by hand, is the same arithmetic but only until someone edits the loop.
//
// Apply is separate from Resolve because the two things that must stay sharp at the composition's
// own resolution are the scope mask and the layer's mask chain. Gating and blending at full
// resolution keeps a scoped runoff a crisp cut through a smooth streak rather than a smooth cut.
//
// Four entry points rather than one with a Mode uniform, and that is also deliberate. A mode
// branch means every pass binds every output slot, so three of the four outputs on any given
// dispatch were one-by-one stand-ins written by nothing -- protected only by a branch that was
// assumed not to be taken, while the code inside it still addressed those stand-ins with
// full-resolution pixel coordinates. Separate entry points give each pass a parameter struct
// holding exactly the resources it writes, so the protection is that the binding does not exist.

#include "/Engine/Public/Platform.ush"
#include "/Plugin/Mixtormat/Private/MixtormatMaskOps.ush"
#include "/Plugin/Mixtormat/Private/MixtormatDebugColor.ush"

// The grid this dispatch writes, always. Every pass bounds-checks against it and derives its UV
// from it, so no entry point can address its output with another pass's resolution.
int2 OutputSize;

// The full composition resolution, and only the downsample needs it: it is the grid being read,
// not the grid being written.
int2 SourceSize;
int DownsampleTaps;

uint Initialize;
uint SurfaceValid;
uint UseLayerMask;
uint BlendMode;
uint Seed;
int StrataCount;

float GravityAngle;
float StreakRadius;
float StreakSoftness;
float SurfaceInfluence;
float StrataAmount;
float WarpScale;
float WarpAmount;
float LipStrength;
float Strength;

// Full resolution.
Texture2D<float> SourceHeight;
Texture2D<float> PreviousMask;
Texture2D<float> FeatureMask;

// Analysis resolution. Height in x, the layer's accumulated mask in y.
Texture2D<float2> AnalysisSource;

// Analysis resolution. Source coverage in x, the raw fractal field in y.
Texture2D<float2> RunoffSource;

// Analysis resolution. The resolved streak with its lip, before scope and Strength.
Texture2D<float> ResolvedRunoff;

SamplerState LinearWrapSampler;

RWTexture2D<float2> OutputAnalysis;
RWTexture2D<float2> OutputSource;
RWTexture2D<float> OutputRunoff;
RWTexture2D<float> OutputMask;

#if MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_WRITE_DEBUG
RWTexture2D<float4> OutputDebug;
#endif

// The reference the CPU already divided StreakRadius by. Here only to turn a UV length back into
// a sane tap floor, so a step never collapses to nothing on a very short run.
static const float MixtormatRunoffReferenceResolution = 1024.0f;

// Taps per stratum group, and the number that decides whether this is cheap.
//
// Fixed, and spread across the reach in UV rather than one per texel. That is what keeps the
// effect the same size at 1K, 2K and 4K: a texel-spaced walk would need four times as many taps
// at 4K to cover the same fraction of the texture, and capping it there would silently shorten
// every streak instead of merely sampling it more coarsely. Spreading a constant count over the
// reach trades sampling density for scale stability, which is the right way round -- the source
// field being smeared is already low-frequency, so the density is what can afford to give.
#define MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_TAPS 24

// Strata are unrolled against this, not against StrataCount, which is dynamic.
#define MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_MAX_STRATA 5

// Minus half of log2(e), so the Gaussian folds its own -0.5 into the exponent constant and reaches
// exp2 directly. This is the lowering every compiler performs on exp() anyway; writing it out
// saves the multiply that would otherwise sit inside the tap loop a hundred and twenty times per
// pixel, and makes it visible that nothing transcendental is being paid for twice.
#define MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_GAUSSIAN_EXPONENT -0.7213475204444817f

float MixtormatRunoffHash(float2 Cell, uint InSeed)
{
	// Integer lattice in, one value out. The seed is folded in as a coordinate rather than as a
	// multiplier so that two nearby seeds give unrelated fields instead of scaled ones.
	float3 P = float3(Cell, (float)(InSeed & 0xFFFFu) * 0.618034f);
	P = frac(P * float3(0.1031f, 0.1030f, 0.0973f));
	P += dot(P, P.yzx + 33.33f);
	return frac((P.x + P.y) * P.z);
}

// Value noise on a wrapped integer lattice.
//
// Tileable by construction: the lattice coordinate is taken modulo the period before it is
// hashed, so the cell at the right edge and the cell at the left edge are the same cell. That is
// the whole reason this is value noise over a period rather than any of the prettier gradient
// constructions -- the effect has to survive being laid next to itself, and a noise that only
// almost tiles shows up as a seam down the middle of every streak.
float MixtormatRunoffValueNoise(float2 UV, float Period, uint InSeed)
{
	const float SafePeriod = max(floor(Period), 1.0f);
	const float2 P = UV * SafePeriod;
	const float2 Cell = floor(P);
	float2 F = frac(P);

	// Quintic, so the second derivative is continuous at the lattice lines. Cubic leaves a faint
	// grid visible once the field is used as a displacement, which is exactly what it is used for.
	F = F * F * F * (F * (F * 6.0f - 15.0f) + 10.0f);

	const float2 C00 = fmod(Cell + float2(0.0f, 0.0f), SafePeriod);
	const float2 C10 = fmod(Cell + float2(1.0f, 0.0f), SafePeriod);
	const float2 C01 = fmod(Cell + float2(0.0f, 1.0f), SafePeriod);
	const float2 C11 = fmod(Cell + float2(1.0f, 1.0f), SafePeriod);

	const float V00 = MixtormatRunoffHash(C00, InSeed);
	const float V10 = MixtormatRunoffHash(C10, InSeed);
	const float V01 = MixtormatRunoffHash(C01, InSeed);
	const float V11 = MixtormatRunoffHash(C11, InSeed);

	return lerp(lerp(V00, V10, F.x), lerp(V01, V11, F.x), F.y);
}

// Three octaves, roughness 0.5, lacunarity 2. The fractal idea from the Houdini reference kept
// and nothing else: no animation, no dimensionality, no post-processing, no channel count. An
// octave doubles the period and halves the weight, and the sum is normalised back to 0..1 so the
// downstream smoothstep thresholds mean the same thing whatever the octave count becomes.
//
// Three because the field does two jobs, and neither wants more. As breakup it is multiplied into
// a source that is about to be smeared over hundreds of texels, so detail finer than the smear is
// detail the smear deletes. As warp it displaces a whole stratum, and a warp field with fine
// octaves in it makes a stratum ragged rather than uneven.
float MixtormatRunoffFBM(float2 UV, float Period, uint InSeed)
{
	float Sum = MixtormatRunoffValueNoise(UV, Period, InSeed);
	float Weight = 1.0f;
	float Total = 1.0f;
	float OctavePeriod = Period;

	[unroll]
	for (int Octave = 1; Octave < 3; ++Octave)
	{
		Weight *= 0.5f;
		OctavePeriod *= 2.0f;
		Sum += MixtormatRunoffValueNoise(UV, OctavePeriod, InSeed + (uint)Octave * 131u) * Weight;
		Total += Weight;
	}

	return Sum / Total;
}

// The direction runoff flows, as a unit vector in UV.
//
// Sin is negated because the angle is authored the way a person reads a screen -- -90 is down --
// while V grows downward in texture space, so the two disagree by a flip. Getting this backwards
// is invisible in the code and extremely visible on a wall.
//
// It also has to agree with the rest of the plugin: Stain treats negative Gravity as flow toward
// +V, and its default is negative. Runoff's default of -90 resolves to (0, +1) here, which is the
// same direction. Two effects on one layer streaking opposite ways from the same defaults would
// be indefensible.
float2 MixtormatRunoffGravity()
{
	return float2(cos(GravityAngle), -sin(GravityAngle));
}

// The height, off the analysis grid. Every read of the surface in this shader goes through here,
// and it reads the reduced copy rather than the composition's own height, so the cavity rings hit
// one small resident texture instead of scattering across a full-resolution one.
float SampleHeight(float2 UV)
{
	return AnalysisSource.SampleLevel(LinearWrapSampler, UV, 0.0f).x;
}

// The three scales concavity is measured at, in reference texels.
//
// Three rather than one because a cavity has a size, and a test tuned to one size is blind to the
// others. The first version of this was a single-texel Laplacian -- centre against the mean of its
// four neighbours -- which is a fine detector for a mortar line and completely blind to a bowl:
// the middle of a broad recess is locally almost planar, so the Laplacian there is zero and the
// dish read as flat ground. Only the ledges showed up, because a ledge is a first derivative and
// first derivatives do not care about scale in the same way.
//
// Two texels catches a crease, eight catches a moulding or a panel gap, thirty-two catches an
// actual recess. The widest is deliberately close to the default streak radius: a cavity too broad
// to source across is a cavity the runoff cannot cross anyway.
//
// Reference texels, against 1024, which is also the resolution the analysis grid targets. The two
// agreeing is not a coincidence and is worth stating: a two-reference-texel ring is two actual
// texels on the analysis grid, so the finest scale this detector was ever tuned to find is exactly
// the finest scale the grid can carry. Running the rings at 4K was measuring below the tuning.
static const float MixtormatRunoffCavityRadii[3] = { 2.0f, 8.0f, 32.0f };

// Height difference at which a feature counts as fully sourcing, against a 0..1 height.
//
// Lower than the prototype's 35 and 22, and the two are now comparable to each other, which
// matters more than either absolute. At 35 against a one-texel Laplacian the cavity term needed a
// near-vertical crease to register anything, while the ledge term at 22 saturated on almost any
// slope -- so summing them and clamping meant the ledge decided the answer everywhere the two
// overlapped. Measured over a ring instead, the same feature produces a far larger difference, so
// the gain comes down and the two terms can both be visible in one result.
#define MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_CAVITY_GAIN 10.0f
#define MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_LEDGE_GAIN 8.0f

// Mean height on a ring of eight taps. Axes and diagonals, evenly weighted.
//
// A ring rather than a disc: what the concavity test wants to know is how the surroundings sit
// relative to here, and the interior of the disc is mostly answering about points nearer than the
// scale being measured -- which the smaller ring has already answered.
float RingMeanHeight(float2 UV, float RadiusUV)
{
	const float Diagonal = RadiusUV * 0.70710678f;
	float Sum = SampleHeight(UV + float2(RadiusUV, 0.0f));
	Sum += SampleHeight(UV + float2(-RadiusUV, 0.0f));
	Sum += SampleHeight(UV + float2(0.0f, RadiusUV));
	Sum += SampleHeight(UV + float2(0.0f, -RadiusUV));
	Sum += SampleHeight(UV + float2(Diagonal, Diagonal));
	Sum += SampleHeight(UV + float2(Diagonal, -Diagonal));
	Sum += SampleHeight(UV + float2(-Diagonal, Diagonal));
	Sum += SampleHeight(UV + float2(-Diagonal, -Diagonal));
	return Sum * 0.125f;
}

// Where runoff is allowed to start.
//
// Two questions, both asked of the height accumulated below the layer. Is this texel sitting in a
// dip, at any of three scales -- dirt collects in dips. And is the ground about to fall away
// downstream of it -- that is a ledge, and a ledge is where a run begins.
//
// Both are measured at radii fixed against the reference resolution rather than in actual texels,
// for the same reason Streak Radius is converted on the CPU: a one-texel measurement finds a
// quarter of the height difference at 4K that it finds at 1K, so a surface response built on
// texel spacing would quietly fade out as the composition grew. Sampling at a UV radius asks the
// same question of the same feature at every resolution.
float SurfaceResponseAt(float2 UV)
{
	if (SurfaceValid == 0)
	{
		// Nothing composited underneath yet, so there is no relief to read. Fully responsive
		// rather than fully dead: on the bottom layer the incoming mask is the entire source,
		// which is the sane reading of "runoff from what you painted".
		return 1.0f;
	}

	const float HeightCentre = SampleHeight(UV);

	// Max across the scales, not a sum. Each ring is the same question asked at a different size,
	// so the answers are alternatives rather than contributions -- a feature that registers at one
	// scale is a cavity, and summing would make a feature that happens to register at two of them
	// twice the cavity of one that registers at three.
	float Cavity = 0.0f;
	[unroll]
	for (int Ring = 0; Ring < 3; ++Ring)
	{
		const float RadiusUV =
			MixtormatRunoffCavityRadii[Ring] / MixtormatRunoffReferenceResolution;
		const float Mean = RingMeanHeight(UV, RadiusUV);
		Cavity = max(
			Cavity,
			saturate((Mean - HeightCentre) * MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_CAVITY_GAIN));
	}

	// Downhill along gravity, not along -Y: turning the gravity dial has to turn what counts as
	// downhill with it, or a sideways runoff would still be sourcing from horizontal ledges.
	//
	// Measured at the middle cavity radius rather than one texel away. A ledge worth streaking
	// from is a drop of some size, and asking at a scale keeps this term reading the same feature
	// the cavity rings read rather than every faint gradient in the height.
	const float LedgeRadiusUV =
		MixtormatRunoffCavityRadii[1] / MixtormatRunoffReferenceResolution;
	const float HeightAhead = SampleHeight(UV + MixtormatRunoffGravity() * LedgeRadiusUV);
	const float Ledge = saturate((HeightCentre - HeightAhead) * MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_LEDGE_GAIN);

	// Lerp toward one rather than scaling toward zero, which is where this departs from the
	// prototype's `saturate((cavity + ledge) * influence)`. Under that expression Surface
	// Influence 0 produces no source at all, making it a second, worse Strength; here it means
	// "ignore the height", so the incoming mask alone drives the runoff. That is the reading the
	// control's name promises and the one that is actually useful -- streaking from a painted
	// mark on a flat surface is a thing people want.
	return lerp(1.0f, saturate(Cavity + Ledge), saturate(SurfaceInfluence));
}

// Pass one. The composition's height and mask, reduced onto the analysis grid.
//
// One bilinear tap already averages a two-by-two block of source texels, so reducing by a factor
// of D needs D/2 taps per axis and not one more. At D of one or two that is a single tap on the
// analysis texel's own centre, which lands exactly on a source texel centre and on a source
// two-by-two midpoint respectively -- so a 1K composition, where the divisor is one, reads its
// height and mask through this pass completely unchanged rather than through a filter.
//
// A box filter rather than a point sample because everything downstream reads this twenty-six
// times per texel at three radii. Point-sampling would hand the cavity rings aliased 4K detail
// and make the surface response at 4K disagree with the same response at 1K on the same material,
// which is the one thing the reference-texel radii exist to prevent.
[numthreads(8, 8, 1)]
void DownsampleCS(uint3 DispatchThreadId : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	if (any(DispatchThreadId.xy >= uint2(OutputSize)))
	{
		return;
	}

	const int2 Pixel = int2(DispatchThreadId.xy);
	const float2 UV = (float2(Pixel) + 0.5f) / float2(OutputSize);

	const int Taps = max(DownsampleTaps, 1);
	const float2 TapStep = 2.0f / float2(max(SourceSize, int2(1, 1)));
	const float TapCentre = (float)(Taps - 1) * 0.5f;

	float Height = 0.0f;
	float Mask = 0.0f;

	[loop]
	for (int Y = 0; Y < Taps; ++Y)
	{
		[loop]
		for (int X = 0; X < Taps; ++X)
		{
			const float2 TapUV =
				UV + (float2((float)X, (float)Y) - TapCentre) * TapStep;
			Height += SourceHeight.SampleLevel(LinearWrapSampler, TapUV, 0.0f);
			Mask += PreviousMask.SampleLevel(LinearWrapSampler, TapUV, 0.0f);
		}
	}

	const float Normalization = 1.0f / (float)(Taps * Taps);

	// The layer's accumulated mask, and only from the second mask child onward. The read half is
	// cleared to white, so trusting it on the first child would source runoff over every texel in
	// the composition instead of falling back to the surface's own shape.
	OutputAnalysis[Pixel] = float2(
		Height * Normalization,
		UseLayerMask != 0 ? saturate(Mask * Normalization) : 1.0f);
}

// Pass two. Everything that is invariant across the smear, resolved once.
[numthreads(8, 8, 1)]
void PrepareCS(uint3 DispatchThreadId : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	if (any(DispatchThreadId.xy >= uint2(OutputSize)))
	{
		return;
	}

	const int2 Pixel = int2(DispatchThreadId.xy);
	const float2 UV = (float2(Pixel) + 0.5f) / float2(OutputSize);

	const float LayerMask =
		saturate(AnalysisSource.SampleLevel(LinearWrapSampler, UV, 0.0f).y);

	const float Noise = MixtormatRunoffFBM(UV, WarpScale, Seed);

	// Breakup, at a fixed 45%. Enough that a default runoff arrives already uneven -- a streak
	// with no grain in it reads as a gradient, not as dirt -- and not so much that the source
	// stops following the surface it was derived from. Exposing the blend would be a sixth
	// control for something with one good value.
	const float Breakup = smoothstep(0.28f, 0.72f, Noise);

	const float Source = LayerMask * SurfaceResponseAt(UV) * lerp(1.0f, Breakup, 0.45f);

	// Source in x, the raw noise in y. The noise is carried forward rather than recomputed in the
	// resolve because the resolve wants it for the per-stratum warp, and re-evaluating three
	// octaves per pixel to avoid one texture read would be the wrong trade in both directions.
	OutputSource[Pixel] = float2(saturate(Source), Noise);
}

// Pass three. One walk up the gravity axis, shared by every stratum.
//
// The loop is over taps, not over strata-times-taps, which is the other half of why this is
// cheap. A tap fetches the source once at its distance upstream; each stratum then decides what
// that sample is worth to it from its own reach, warp and lip -- pure arithmetic on a value that
// is already in a register. Five strata cost five multiply-adds per tap, not five texture reads.
//
// Every reciprocal in that arithmetic is hoisted. The reach a stratum gets from a tap is the
// tap's source times that stratum's maximum, and both the Gaussian argument and the lip's
// normalised distance are that reach divided into a warped distance -- so one reciprocal of the
// source per tap and one reciprocal of the maximum per stratum replace two divides per tap per
// stratum, which at twenty-four by five was two hundred and forty divides per pixel.
[numthreads(8, 8, 1)]
void ResolveCS(uint3 DispatchThreadId : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	if (any(DispatchThreadId.xy >= uint2(OutputSize)))
	{
		return;
	}

	const int2 Pixel = int2(DispatchThreadId.xy);
	const float2 UV = (float2(Pixel) + 0.5f) / float2(OutputSize);

	// Automated strata shaping. Strata Amount is one dial over four behaviours because they only
	// make sense together: widening the spacing without spreading the lengths just moves the
	// stack, and flattening the opacity without either makes it uniformly muddier. Separating
	// them would be four controls with one useful diagonal through them.
	const float SafeStrataAmount = saturate(StrataAmount);
	const float StrataSpacing = lerp(0.55f, 1.15f, SafeStrataAmount);
	const float StrataLengthStep = 0.08f + 0.04f * SafeStrataAmount;
	const float StrataOpacityFalloff = lerp(1.15f, 0.75f, SafeStrataAmount);

	// A soft run deposits over a longer stretch than a sharp one, so the terminal lip widens with
	// the same control that softens the streak. Derived rather than exposed for that reason: a
	// lip width that did not track softness would need re-tuning every time the streak moved.
	const float LipWidth = 0.35f + saturate(StreakSoftness) * 0.35f;
	const float InvLipWidth = 1.0f / LipWidth;

	const float2 Gravity = MixtormatRunoffGravity();
	const float SafeSoftness = max(StreakSoftness, 0.05f);
	const float InvSoftness = 1.0f / SafeSoftness;

	// The noise this pixel warps by, read once. Three decorrelated values derived from it by the
	// cheap frac trick in the brief, so the strata do not all deform identically without paying
	// for three more fractal evaluations.
	const float BaseNoise = RunoffSource.SampleLevel(LinearWrapSampler, UV, 0.0f).y;
	const float NoiseA = BaseNoise;
	const float NoiseB = frac(BaseNoise * 1.73f + 0.31f);
	const float NoiseC = frac(BaseNoise * 2.17f + 0.57f);

	// Contrast shaping on the warp field, tightened as the warp grows. A large displacement
	// through a soft field smears every stratum the same way; through a contrasted one it pushes
	// some of them hard and leaves others, which is what makes the stack read as separate runs.
	const float WarpContrast = clamp(0.35f - WarpAmount * 0.10f, 0.12f, 0.35f);
	const float WarpMask = max(
		smoothstep(0.5f - WarpContrast, 0.5f + WarpContrast, NoiseA),
		smoothstep(0.5f - WarpContrast, 0.5f + WarpContrast, max(NoiseB, NoiseC)));
	const float WarpSigned = WarpMask * 2.0f - 1.0f;

	const int SafeStrataCount = clamp(StrataCount, 2, MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_MAX_STRATA);
	const float StrataDivisor = (float)max(SafeStrataCount - 1, 1);

	// Per-stratum constants, hoisted out of the tap loop. Only the three the loop actually reads
	// are kept as arrays; the reach, the start offset and the warp displacement that produce them
	// are locals inside the setup, so they do not hold registers across twenty-four iterations.
	// That matters more than the arithmetic it saves: this loop's occupancy is what decides
	// whether the source fetches hide behind anything.
	float WarpBias[MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_MAX_STRATA];
	float InvMaxReach[MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_MAX_STRATA];
	float Opacity[MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_MAX_STRATA];
	float Accumulated[MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_MAX_STRATA];
	float WeightSum[MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_MAX_STRATA];
	float LipAccumulated[MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_MAX_STRATA];

	float FurthestReach = 0.0f;
	float OpacityTotal = 0.0f;

	[unroll]
	for (int Init = 0; Init < MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_MAX_STRATA; ++Init)
	{
		const float LayerT = (float)Init / StrataDivisor;

		// Later strata start further down and run longer, so their heads overlap the tails of the
		// ones before them. That overlap is the layered look: strata that all started together
		// would differ only in length and read as one run with a soft end.
		const float MaxReach =
			StreakRadius * (0.85f + LayerT * StrataLengthStep * (float)SafeStrataCount);
		const float StartOffset = StrataSpacing * (float)Init * StreakRadius * 0.12f;

		// Vertical only. The displacement is a scalar on the distance along gravity, never a
		// vector -- there is no expression anywhere in this shader that can move a sample off the
		// gravity axis, which is what "no arbitrary sideways flow" has to mean to be true rather
		// than merely intended.
		//
		// The per-stratum multiplier alternates sign so adjacent strata pull apart rather than
		// drifting together, and grows with the index so the stack fans out down its length.
		const float LayerMultiplier =
			(Init % 2 == 0 ? 1.0f : -1.0f) * (0.35f + LayerT * 0.65f);
		const float WarpOffset =
			WarpSigned * WarpAmount * StreakRadius * LayerMultiplier * 0.25f;

		// The tap loop only ever wants the difference of the two offsets and the reciprocal of
		// the maximum, so those are what it is handed.
		WarpBias[Init] = WarpOffset - StartOffset;
		InvMaxReach[Init] = 1.0f / max(MaxReach, 1.0e-5f);

		Opacity[Init] = 1.0f / (1.0f + (float)Init * StrataOpacityFalloff);
		Accumulated[Init] = 0.0f;
		WeightSum[Init] = 0.0f;
		LipAccumulated[Init] = 0.0f;

		if (Init < SafeStrataCount)
		{
			FurthestReach = max(FurthestReach, MaxReach + abs(WarpOffset) + StartOffset);
			OpacityTotal += Opacity[Init];
		}
	}

	// The walk covers the furthest stratum. Beyond its reach every Gaussian has decayed past
	// anything a 16-bit mask can hold, so there is nothing further upstream worth fetching.
	const float Step = max(FurthestReach / (float)MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_TAPS,
		0.5f / MixtormatRunoffReferenceResolution);

	// Sigma cannot go below roughly half a reference texel or a tight streak turns into a line of
	// disconnected taps at the moment it stops being able to resolve itself. Applied as a ceiling
	// on the reciprocal rather than a floor on sigma, which is the same clamp without the divide:
	// the Gaussian argument is a non-negative distance over sigma, so the larger sigma gives the
	// smaller argument and the floor becomes a min.
	const float InvMinSigma = MixtormatRunoffReferenceResolution / 0.5f;

	[loop]
	for (int Tap = 0; Tap < MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_TAPS; ++Tap)
	{
		// Half a step in, so tap zero samples the middle of its own band rather than exactly the
		// output pixel, which would double-count the source against the first stratum.
		const float Distance = ((float)Tap + 0.5f) * Step;

		// Upstream: runoff at this pixel came from somewhere against gravity, so the source is
		// fetched back along the axis. Wrapped by the sampler, which is what keeps a streak that
		// runs off the bottom edge arriving at the top.
		const float2 TapUV = UV - Gravity * Distance;
		const float Source = RunoffSource.SampleLevel(LinearWrapSampler, TapUV, 0.0f).x;

		if (Source <= 0.0f)
		{
			continue;
		}

		// One reciprocal for every stratum's reach at this tap. Mask intensity is a length
		// control, not only an opacity: a strong source runs the full radius, a weak one fades
		// out early, from the same settings. This is what makes the incoming mask feel like it is
		// driving the effect rather than dimming it, and it is why the reach has to be evaluated
		// per tap -- the value it depends on is the source at the tap, not at the pixel being
		// written.
		const float InvSource = 1.0f / Source;

		[unroll]
		for (int Layer = 0; Layer < MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_MAX_STRATA; ++Layer)
		{
			// Wave-uniform, because the count comes from a uniform: the hardware skips the block
			// for an inactive stratum rather than predicating through it. The unrolled five cost
			// their registers whatever the count is, which is why the arrays above were trimmed,
			// but they do not cost their arithmetic.
			if (Layer >= SafeStrataCount)
			{
				continue;
			}

			const float Warped = max(Distance + WarpBias[Layer], 0.0f);

			// The reciprocal of the reach, ceilinged where the original floored the reach itself
			// at 1e-5. The same clamp, as two multiplies and a min instead of a divide.
			const float InvReach = min(InvSource * InvMaxReach[Layer], 1.0e5f);

			// The tidemark's normalised distance, and the Gaussian argument, which is that same
			// number over the softness. Deriving the second from the first is what removes the
			// second divide: sigma is the reach times the softness, so the argument is the
			// normalised distance times the reciprocal softness.
			const float NormalizedDistance = Warped * InvReach;
			const float X = min(NormalizedDistance * InvSoftness, Warped * InvMinSigma);
			const float Gaussian = exp2(MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_GAUSSIAN_EXPONENT * X * X);

			Accumulated[Layer] += Source * Gaussian;
			WeightSum[Layer] += Gaussian;

			// The tidemark, where the run stops. Measured against the stratum's own reach so it
			// tracks wherever the warp put the end, rather than sitting at a fixed distance that
			// the warp would then walk the streak away from. Written out rather than left to
			// smoothstep so the reciprocal of the width is hoisted with the rest of them.
			const float LipT = saturate(abs(NormalizedDistance - 1.0f) * InvLipWidth);
			const float Edge = 1.0f - LipT * LipT * (3.0f - 2.0f * LipT);
			LipAccumulated[Layer] += Source * Edge;
		}
	}

	float Runoff = 0.0f;
	float Lip = 0.0f;

	[unroll]
	for (int Combine = 0; Combine < MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_MAX_STRATA; ++Combine)
	{
		if (Combine >= SafeStrataCount)
		{
			continue;
		}

		// Against the Gaussian weight rather than the tap count: a normalised smear of a solid
		// source is that source, whatever the sampling happened to be. Dividing by the taps
		// instead would make the result depend on the step, and the step depends on the reach.
		const float Normalized = Accumulated[Combine] / max(WeightSum[Combine], 1.0e-5f);
		Runoff = max(Runoff, Normalized * Opacity[Combine]);
		Lip = max(Lip, saturate(LipAccumulated[Combine] * 0.5f) * Opacity[Combine]);
	}

	// Max across strata, then normalised against the opacity a single full stratum would have
	// carried. Max rather than sum because the strata are overlapping readings of one runoff, not
	// separate deposits stacked on top of each other -- summing them would make the shared head of
	// the stack, where every stratum agrees, blow out to white while the tails stayed faint. The
	// normalisation is what stops a longer radius, which buys more strata, from also brightening
	// the whole mask.
	const float StrataNormalization = max(OpacityTotal / (float)SafeStrataCount, 1.0e-5f);
	Runoff = saturate(Runoff / StrataNormalization);
	Lip = saturate(Lip / StrataNormalization);

	OutputRunoff[Pixel] = saturate(Runoff + Lip * saturate(LipStrength));
}

// Pass four. The resolved streak into the layer's mask chain, at the composition's resolution.
//
// Three reads and a blend. The streak arrives filtered up off the analysis grid, which it can
// afford to be -- it is a Gaussian smear whose sigma is a substantial fraction of the texture --
// while the scope mask and the incoming chain are read where they actually live, so neither is
// softened by the reduction that made the smear cheap.
[numthreads(8, 8, 1)]
void ApplyCS(uint3 DispatchThreadId : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	if (any(DispatchThreadId.xy >= uint2(OutputSize)))
	{
		return;
	}

	const int2 Pixel = int2(DispatchThreadId.xy);
	const float2 UV = (float2(Pixel) + 0.5f) / float2(OutputSize);

	// Initialize is the first mask child on the layer: there is no accumulated mask yet, and the
	// half bound here is cleared to white rather than to what the layer had. Every mask node in
	// the plugin treats Previous as zero in that case, and a runoff that trusted the white would
	// blend against full coverage and resolve to nothing.
	const float Previous = Initialize != 0
		? 0.0f
		: saturate(PreviousMask.SampleLevel(LinearWrapSampler, UV, 0.0f));

	const float WithLip = saturate(ResolvedRunoff.SampleLevel(LinearWrapSampler, UV, 0.0f));

	// The scoped feature mask gates the resolved runoff without becoming part of the layer's
	// accumulated mask chain. Applied at the end, so a scoped runoff is the same streak with the
	// outside cut away rather than a different, shorter streak -- gating the source instead would
	// let runoff stop at the scope boundary and never reach past it.
	const float Scope = saturate(FeatureMask.SampleLevel(LinearWrapSampler, UV, 0.0f));
	const float Incoming = WithLip * Scope;

	const float Blended = MixtormatApplyMaskOperation(BlendMode, Previous, Incoming);
	OutputMask[Pixel] = saturate(lerp(Previous, Blended, saturate(Strength)));

	// The runoff before Strength blends it into the chain, matching what every other feature
	// preview in the plugin shows: the same unlit ramp, so runoff reads as one more channel view
	// rather than as its own kind of display. Compiled out entirely on the permutation that is
	// not previewing, so the shared debug target is neither bound nor addressable from here.
#if MIXTORMAT_RUNOFF_WRITE_DEBUG
	OutputDebug[Pixel] = float4(MixtormatDebugRamp(Incoming), 1.0f);
#endif
}
